//! SOS kernel core: the arch-free kernel.
//!
//! Everything a SOS kernel image shares: the console, the trap handler `ktrap`,
//! the object-op dispatch, and the sosimg loader. An image is one per-arch
//! kernel HAL (`hal/<arch>/kernel/`) + the shared runtime seams
//! (`sosrt`, rt/common/src/lib.saw) + this module + one entry `.saw` defining
//! `kmain` — `kernel/main.saw` for the real kernel, `tests/*.saw` for
//! the harness cases. Sharing the module is what keeps every test image on the
//! same trap path instead of a copy of it.
//!
//! No architecture appears in this directory. Registers, trap causes,
//! protection hardware, the board's console and the way the machine stops are
//! all reached through `hal`, and the harness enforces it: a scan rejects an
//! arch name anywhere under `core/`, so the seam cannot erode by one convenient
//! constant at a time. Adding an architecture is writing a HAL.
//!
//! Addresses and machine words are `UInt` throughout, never `Int`: `Int` is
//! platform-width and signed, so a RAM address lands in its negative half on a
//! 32-bit target and means a different number on a 64-bit one.
//!
//! # This file is the facade
//!
//! This file holds no code: it re-exports the names an image entry uses, so
//! `import kcore.{console, load_root_and_enter}` in `main.saw` and in every
//! `tests/` entry resolves here.
//!
//! The modules, in dependency order. A file may import only those above it,
//! because an import cycle is not diagnosed; it silently empties a module's
//! export table and blames a third file.
//!
//!   `limits`     every slab size, the frame stride, the "no slot" sentinels
//!   `mem`        addresses and byte moves
//!   `diag`       the console, and the reports that stop the machine
//!   `stats`      the published trap-counter region
//!   `result`     a syscall's answer, and the one place it enters a frame
//!   `slab`       the extent chain every donatable kind is held in
//!   `pslot`      the process record and body and their slabs — the quota
//!                ledger is the record's, the handle table, the boot set and
//!                the grant record the body's
//!   `objects`    the handle-table operations, the validated per-kind objects,
//!                the Memory slab and the boot-handle queue's operations
//!   `threads`    the thread slab, the frame arena, the ready queue
//!   `time`       the Clock/Timer slabs and the one hardware comparator
//!   `process`    the grant record, the copy doors, the way into user mode
//!   `waitables`  the Event/Waiter/Interrupt slabs and the per-kind matrix
//!   `refs`       the reference count, free-on-zero, the per-kind last rites,
//!                and the delivery that turns readiness into a woken thread
//!   `irq`        the tick, expiry, device lines, the idle path, the point
//!   `preempt`    the strided long-op movers that take that point
//!   `loader`     the sosimg loader — validate, place, record
//!   `sched`      the switch point, and the end of a process
//!   `dispatch`   every object op, and `ktrap`
//!
//! A module sits at the altitude its call graph allows, and where a piece lives
//! is decided by what it has to reach. The state of a thing sits below the code
//! that acts on it: ending a process (`sched`) touches every slab, and
//! answering a wait (`refs`) goes through `process`'s copy door. That is why a
//! thread's table (`threads`) is not its scheduler (`sched`), a timer's
//! schedule (`time`) is not its expiry (`irq`), and the wait tables
//! (`waitables`) are not the delivery (`refs`).
//!
//!   - The teardown is in `sched`, not `process`. Ending a process can
//!     reschedule, a reschedule can idle, and idling delivers, which reaches
//!     back down into `process`'s own copy door; written in `process` it would
//!     be an import cycle. The visible cost is that the copy doors report a bad
//!     buffer and their callers terminate on it. `sched.saw`'s header has the
//!     argument.
//!   - The loader sits just below `sched`. It has two doors, boot and
//!     `process_create` (an op), so its phases have to be reachable from
//!     `dispatch`. It reaches nothing above itself.
//!   - `preempt` sits above `irq` because a preemption point delivers, while
//!     the byte loops it drives sit at the bottom under `mem` (delivery reaches
//!     them through `refs` -> `process` -> `copy_out`). A point inside the loop
//!     would make `mem` import `irq`. `preempt.saw`'s header has the argument.
//!   - `pslot` holds every table a process index keys (handle table, quota
//!     ledger, boot set, grant record), as fields of the record or the body,
//!     because a donatable slab is one array keyed by its slot and a parallel
//!     table is one a donated slot does not have. It sits below `objects`,
//!     whose handle functions index it, and below `process`. The functions
//!     that act on that state sit above it: `mint_handle` is in `objects` and
//!     the grant-record operations are in `process`.
//!   - `stats` holds the published trap-counter region, at the bottom: it names
//!     only `mem`, `diag` and the HAL, and nothing it calls can reach back into
//!     it. Its callers are spread across the order (the trap path and the stats
//!     op in `dispatch`, the create reset in `process`, the boot-time publish
//!     in `loader`), so it cannot live in any one of them.
//!   - `waitables` sits above `pslot`, `threads` and `time`, whose slabs its
//!     per-kind matrix reads: a Process handle is a waitable, so the matrix's
//!     Process arms read `PROCESS_RECORDS`. It does not import `process`, and
//!     `process` must not import it.
//!   - Delivery lives in `refs`. A delivery drops a counted reference (a
//!     one-shot attachment detaches when its record is copied out) and a
//!     dropped reference delivers (a column reaching zero raises the peer-gone
//!     terminal levels, and `drop_reference` is the only place that is
//!     observed). The two are mutually recursive, so they cannot be separate
//!     modules. `refs.saw`'s header has the argument.
//!
//! The cost of the split: Saw has no package-internal visibility, so every name
//! two seams share is `public`, visible to anything that imports `kcore`, not
//! merely to its siblings. The kernel's own tables are not protected from the
//! kernel's own consumers by the language. The image is unaffected; the
//! widening is a compile-time fact.

public import kcore.mem.{align_up}
public import kcore.diag.{Console, ExitCode, console, fatal, fatal_image}
public import kcore.time.{start_tick}
public import kcore.process.{start_process, boot_tick_enabled}
public import kcore.irq.{timer_ticks, enable_tick_report}
public import kcore.threads.{TICK_PERIOD_US}
public import kcore.preempt.{long_copy, long_zero}
public import kcore.dispatch.{ktrap}
public import kcore.loader.{load_root_and_enter}
